\begin{abstract}
Monitoring nonlinear closed-loop systems without physical fault models,
labeled fault samples, or known fault directions requires a structural
solution to a fundamental difficulty: measurements that carry abnormal
information also update the reference against which abnormality is judged.
This paper addresses that difficulty through three connected constructions.
First, a strictly causal attention encoder is coupled with a Takagi--Sugeno
(T--S) fuzzy Koopman model to form a measurement-decoupled normal reference;
a start-indexed residual then provides a single mathematical object serving
free-rollout training, short-horizon monitoring, and long-reference
monitoring. Second, the complete fuzzy Koopman Jacobian is decomposed into
the interpolated local dynamics and the variation of normalized rule
weights, yielding an implementable infinity-norm propagation bound and a
reliable reference horizon without eigenvalue, singular-value,
inverse-matrix, or higher-order derivative computations. Third,
model-internal basis perturbations applied separately to input, measurement,
and lifted coordinates generate finite-horizon residual responses through
forward rollouts; the resulting responses are used to jointly reduce normal
propagation, increase perturbation sensitivity, and separate response
patterns. Online scores are standardized by fuzzy operating region,
controller command, command variation, and reference age, with thresholds
calibrated on held-out normal blocks. Sufficient residual-space conditions
are derived for detection and unique group separation. When the conditions
for a unique decision are not met, the method returns a set of compatible
perturbation groups rather than an unsupported physical fault label.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Attention, fault detection, Koopman operator, normal-data-only learning,
structured perturbation, Takagi--Sugeno fuzzy systems.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{N}{onlinear} closed-loop systems commonly traverse multiple
operating regimes while yielding few, if any, representative fault records.
Learning normal input--output behavior is therefore an attractive monitoring
strategy, yet it introduces a structural obstacle: the same measurements that
form a residual are also used to update the state and model against which that
residual is evaluated. When an abnormal measurement feeds an encoder, an
attention context, or a fuzzy scheduling variable, the normal reference may
drift toward the abnormal trajectory, reducing the very discrepancy that
detection relies upon. This measurement-to-reference contamination creates a
coupled design problem that cannot be resolved by any single module in
isolation.

Koopman operator theory seeks lifted coordinates in which nonlinear dynamics
admit a finite-dimensional linear approximation
\cite{brunton2016,lusch2018}. T--S fuzzy models interpolate local linear
descriptions through normalized firing strengths, providing a principled
framework for multi-regime dynamics \cite{takagi1985,tanaka2001,lam2018}.
Their combination has been investigated in modeling and predictive control
\cite{hu2026}, but these prior efforts target control-oriented prediction
accuracy rather than the diagnostic requirements that arise when neither fault
models nor fault data are available. The combination itself is consequently not
treated as a contribution here. What is new is the operational role assigned to
the T--S structure: it supplies the normal reference, it determines the
finite-horizon error propagation bound through its complete Jacobian, and it
conditions the online monitoring decision on the realized operating region.
Attention is retained to represent long input--output histories compactly, but
its output is prevented from routing new measurements into the normal reference
after the initial anchor is established.

Classical fuzzy fault-detection methods introduce explicit fault or
unknown-input channels and design observers or filters through Lyapunov and
linear-matrix-inequality techniques
\cite{nguang2007,yang2011,thumati2014,zhao2020,ran2022}.
Koopman-based fault diagnosis has similarly exploited learned residual and
parity relations \cite{bakhtiaridoust2023,irani2024}. These formulations are
well suited when a physical fault model or representative fault data are
available. In the setting considered here, however, training data are
exclusively normal, and the physical fault location, direction, and temporal
profile are all unknown. A physical minimum fault gain cannot therefore be
identified; the only structure available for diagnosis consists of the known
input and measurement coordinates together with the coordinates of the learned
lifted model.

This restricted information boundary fuses three difficulties into one
connected design problem. Excluding new measurements from the reference
prevents it from adapting to abnormal observations, but simultaneously exposes
the normal free-rollout drift that one-step teacher forcing would have masked.
Reducing that drift alone is insufficient, because a less sensitive model may
also suppress abnormal residuals. Moreover, an unknown physical fault direction
precludes any justified low-dimensional fault-sensitive projection. The method
must instead control normal propagation, retain the full joint residual, and
learn separable responses solely for explicitly defined model-coordinate
perturbation groups.

The principal contributions are as follows.
\begin{enumerate}
\item A measurement-decoupled attention--Koopman--T--S reference and a
start-indexed residual are constructed. The residual object serves
free-rollout training, short-horizon monitoring, and long-reference
monitoring and yields an exact nonlinear residual recursion, while a
delay-confirmed anchor rule protects the reference from abnormal
measurements.

\item The complete T--S fuzzy Koopman Jacobian is decomposed to expose the
contributions of local dynamics, rule overlap, local-model disparity, and
scheduling sensitivity. An infinity-norm bound, enforced by elementwise
parameterization, supplies a finite-horizon normal residual bound and a
reliable reference horizon without costly or poorly differentiable matrix
operations.

\item Perturbation rollouts applied to the input, measurement, and lifted
coordinates are integrated with normal-propagation learning. The resulting
input-conditioned statistic admits sufficient residual-space detection and
group-separation conditions, and returns a set-valued decision when a unique
group is not supported.
\end{enumerate}

The three contributions are interdependent by construction: the reference
defined in the first contribution is the object whose propagation the second
contribution bounds, and the perturbation responses learned in the third
contribution operate on that same reference through ordinary forward rollouts.
Section~II formalizes the information boundary under which these constructions
must operate and states the three subproblems in precise terms. Section~III
develops the normal model, measurement-decoupled reference, propagation
analysis, and structured response learning. Section~IV turns the frozen
residual and learned patterns into an online detection and isolation decision
rule. Section~V summarizes the results and their scope.

