\section{Preliminaries and Problem Formulation}
\subsection{Information Boundary and Controlled-Process Description}
Consider a closed-loop nonlinear system in which the controller issues a
command $\bm u_k\in\mathbb R^{m_u}$ at each sample $k$. The actuator output
$\bm u_k^{\rm a}\in\mathbb R^{m_u}$ actually applied to the controlled process
need not be measured, and may differ from the command because of actuator-side
deviations. The fault-inclusive controlled process is described by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm u_k^{\rm a}&=\bm u_k+\bm f_k^{\rm a},\\
\bm x_{k+1}&=\mathcal X^{\mathfrak n}
(\bm x_k,\bm u_k^{\rm a})
+\bm f_k^{\rm p}+\bm\varepsilon_k^x,\\
\bm y_k&=\mathcal Y^{\mathfrak n}
(\bm x_k,\bm u_k^{\rm a})
+\bm f_k^{\rm s}+\bm\varepsilon_k^y,
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:controlled_process}
\end{equation}
where $\bm x_k\in\mathbb R^{m_x}$ and $\bm y_k\in\mathbb R^{m_y}$ denote the
physical state and measured output, respectively. The additive vectors $\bm
f_k^{\rm a}\in\mathbb R^{m_u}$, $\bm f_k^{\rm p}\in\mathbb R^{m_x}$, and $\bm
f_k^{\rm s}\in\mathbb R^{m_y}$ represent possible actuator-side, process-side,
and measurement-side deviations from normal operation, while $\bm\varepsilon_k^x
\in\mathbb R^{m_x}$ and $\bm\varepsilon_k^y\in\mathbb R^{m_y}$ denote the
disturbances entering the state transition and the output measurement,
respectively. The normal-operation
mappings $\mathcal X^{\mathfrak n}:\mathbb R^{m_x}\times\mathbb R^{m_u}
\rightarrow\mathbb R^{m_x}$ and $\mathcal Y^{\mathfrak n}:\mathbb
R^{m_x}\times\mathbb R^{m_u} \rightarrow\mathbb R^{m_y}$ are unknown. The
dimensions of the fault vectors specify only where a deviation can enter the
physical description; they do not supply known fault directions to the
learning algorithm.

Online fault diagnosis has access only to the recorded command $\bm u_k$ and
the measured output $\bm y_k$. Conditioning the normal model on the recorded
command answers a specific counterfactual question: how would the controlled
process evolve under this command if its actuator and dynamics followed the
normal-operation description? A command that a feedback controller modifies
after sensing an abnormal measurement remains a known conditioning variable;
the unmeasured discrepancy between $\bm u_k^{\rm a}$ and $\bm u_k$ remains an
inconsistency to be detected.

The learning regime is deliberately restricted. Only normal trajectories are
used for model training and threshold calibration; no physical fault matrix,
fault direction, fault derivative, fault time model, or labeled fault
trajectory is employed. All model parameters and calibration quantities are
frozen during testing. To enforce strict causality, the history available at
sample $k$ excludes the current output and is defined as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal H_k^-=
\left\{\bm u_{k-\ell_h:k-1},\,
\bm y_{k-\ell_h:k-1}\right\},
\label{eq:history}
\end{equation}
where $\ell_h$ is the history length.

\subsection{Finite-History Lifted Representation}
The information boundary defined above prohibits access to the physical state
$\bm x_k$ and to the normal-operation mappings $\mathcal X^{\mathfrak n}$,
$\mathcal Y^{\mathfrak n}$. A representation that can be constructed solely
from the available history $\mathcal H_k^-$ is therefore required. Koopman
operator theory provides the conceptual foundation for such a representation,
but the ideal Koopman formulation must be adapted to what the information
boundary permits.

For the normal-operation dynamics, the Koopman operator acts on an observable
by composition with the state transition \cite{brunton2016}. A finite vector
of observables $\bm\psi:\mathbb R^{m_x}\rightarrow\mathbb R^{m_z}$ would
satisfy the ideal relation
\begin{equation}
\bm\psi\!\left(\mathcal X^{\mathfrak n}(\bm x_k,\bm u_k)\right)
=\bm\psi(\bm x_{k+1}).
\label{eq:koopman}
\end{equation}
In practice, a finite invariant subspace may not exist, and the learned lifted
coordinates need not contain the physical state. The present formulation
therefore adopts a predictive coordinate $\bm z_k^{\rm enc}\in\mathbb
R^{m_z}$ estimated from the finite history \eqref{eq:history} and uses a T--S
interpolation of local lifted linear models to approximate \eqref{eq:koopman}
on the normal operating domain. Both the approximation error inherent in
finite lifting and the error due to finite history are retained in the
subsequent propagation analysis.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
Under the information boundary stated above, this paper addresses three
connected subproblems.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Reference construction:} construct a normal reference that admits
new measurements for residual evaluation but not for reference updates, while
using a single residual definition across free-rollout training, short-horizon
monitoring, and long-reference testing.

\item \emph{Normal propagation:} quantify the finite-horizon drift of that
reference through the complete attention--Koopman--T--S mapping using
operations compatible with first-order network training, and make the realized
input and fuzzy operating region explicit in the resulting bound.

\item \emph{Residual-space decision:} using normal data alone, jointly reduce
normal residual propagation and increase the separability of reproducible
model-coordinate perturbation responses; then construct an input-conditioned
detection statistic and return a unique group only when the residual response
provides sufficient evidence.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Proposed Method}
\subsection{Attention--Koopman--T--S Normal Model}
The normal-operation model is built from three components that share a common
information discipline: every learned mapping accesses only the strictly past
history \eqref{eq:history} and the recorded command $\bm u_k$.

Each element of the history is first embedded as a token $\bm h_j$. For
attention head $q\in\{1,\ldots,m_{\rm hd}\}$, the strictly causal attention
coefficient assigned at sample $k$ to a past token $j$ is computed as
\begin{equation}
\varpi_{k,j}^{(q)}
=\frac{\exp\!\left(
(\bm W_Q^{(q)}\bm h_{k-1})^\T
(\bm W_K^{(q)}\bm h_j)/\sqrt{m_{\rm a}}\right)}
{\displaystyle\sum_{\ell=k-\ell_h}^{k-1}
\exp\!\left(
(\bm W_Q^{(q)}\bm h_{k-1})^\T
(\bm W_K^{(q)}\bm h_\ell)/\sqrt{m_{\rm a}}\right)}.
\label{eq:attention}
\end{equation}
The query is formed from the most recent history token $\bm h_{k-1}$, ensuring
that no information from sample $k$ or later enters the attention weights. The
head outputs are concatenated and projected to produce a compact context
vector:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm\chi_k&=\bm W_O
\left[
\sum_{j=k-\ell_h}^{k-1}\varpi_{k,j}^{(q)}
\bm W_V^{(q)}\bm h_j
\right]_{q=1}^{m_{\rm hd}},
\\
\bm z_k^{\rm enc}&=\mathcal H_\Theta(\mathcal H_k^-).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:encoder}
\end{equation}
The encoder $\mathcal H_\Theta$ encompasses the token embeddings and the
multihead attention operation of \eqref{eq:attention}. Attention here serves
to compress the strictly past history; it is not treated as a separate
contribution.

Having formed the encoded state and context, the model couples them with the
controller command to produce a scheduling vector that drives the fuzzy rule
memberships:
\begin{equation}
\bm\rho_k=\bm W_z\bm z_k+\bm W_\chi\bm\chi_k+
\bm W_u\bm u_k.
\label{eq:schedule}
\end{equation}
Constant offsets are absorbed through an augmented constant coordinate.
For each rule $i\in\{1,\ldots,m_r\}$, the negative squared Mahalanobis-like
energy and the normalized firing strength are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\varphi_i(\bm\rho)
&=-\frac{1}{2}(\bm\rho-\bm c_i)^\T
\bm\Pi_i(\bm\rho-\bm c_i),\\
\omega_i(\bm\rho)
&=\frac{\exp(\varphi_i(\bm\rho))}
{\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{m_r}\exp(\varphi_j(\bm\rho))}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:fuzzy_weights}
\end{equation}
By construction, $\omega_i\geq0$ for every rule and $\sum_i\omega_i=1$,
forming a valid convex combination. The diagonal metric $\bm\Pi_i$ and center
$\bm c_i$ of each rule are parameterized with built-in bounds to guarantee
positive definiteness without constrained optimization:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm\Pi_i&=\mathrm{Diag}\!\left[
\underline{\pi}\1+
(\overline{\pi}-\underline{\pi})
\operatorname{sigmoid}(\widetilde{\bm\pi}_i)\right],\\
\bm c_i&=\overline{\bm c}\odot\tanh(\widetilde{\bm c}_i).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:metric_parameterization}
\end{equation}
All operations inside the brackets are elementwise, and the constants satisfy
$0<\underline{\pi}<\overline{\pi}$. Although $\bm\Pi_i$ is diagonal,
cross-coordinate dependence enters through the linear map \eqref{eq:schedule};
a diagonal metric in the scheduling space does not imply an axis-aligned
partition of the original history space.

The local and interpolated lifted dynamics and the output decoder are defined
as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm v_i(\bm z,\bm u)&=\bm A_i\bm z+\bm B_i\bm u,\\
\mathcal T_\Theta(\bm z,\bm u;\bm\chi)
&=\sum_{i=1}^{m_r}\omega_i(\bm\rho)\bm v_i(\bm z,\bm u),\\
\bm y^{\rm dec}&=\mathcal D_\Theta(\bm z,\bm u).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:fuzzy_transition}
\end{equation}
The decoder $\mathcal D_\Theta$ is distinct from the physical output mapping
$\mathcal Y^{\mathfrak n}$; this separation is necessary because the learned
lifted state does not necessarily contain the physical state.

To obtain a computable propagation bound without spectral decomposition, the
matrices that govern the bound are parameterized elementwise through a bounded
hyperbolic-tangent map, which enforces the required bounds by construction.
For example,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
[\bm A_i]_{pq}&=\frac{\kappa_A}{m_z}
\tanh([\widetilde{\bm A}_i]_{pq}),\\
[\bm B_i]_{pq}&=\frac{\kappa_B}{m_u}
\tanh([\widetilde{\bm B}_i]_{pq}),\\
[\bm W_z]_{pq}&=\frac{\kappa_W}{m_z}
\tanh([\widetilde{\bm W}_z]_{pq}).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:hard_bounds}
\end{equation}
Consequently, $\norm{\bm A_i}_\infty\leq\kappa_A$, $\norm{\bm
B_i}_\infty\leq\kappa_B$, and $\norm{\bm W_z}_\infty\leq\kappa_W$ hold by
construction, without requiring spectral projection or post-training
verification.

Training uses a normal data split and a free-rollout horizon $\ell_{\rm tr}$.
Starting from the encoded state at a sampled start $s$, i.e., $\bm
z_{s\mid s}^{\rm roll}=\bm z_s^{\rm enc}$, the model propagates forward,
updating the history from its own predicted outputs rather than from the true
measurements; here $\mathcal H_\Theta^\chi$ denotes the context-producing
component of the encoder $\mathcal H_\Theta$, and $\mathcal H_{t\mid s}^{\rm
roll}$ is the rollout history assembled from self-predicted outputs:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm\chi_{t\mid s}^{\rm roll}
&=\mathcal H_\Theta^\chi(\mathcal H_{t\mid s}^{\rm roll}),\\
\bm z_{t+1\mid s}^{\rm roll}
&=\mathcal T_\Theta(\bm z_{t\mid s}^{\rm roll},\bm u_t;
\bm\chi_{t\mid s}^{\rm roll}),\\
\bm y_{t\mid s}^{\rm roll}
&=\mathcal D_\Theta(\bm z_{t\mid s}^{\rm roll},\bm u_t).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:free_rollout}
\end{equation}
Free rollout, rather than one-step teacher forcing, is essential because the
online measurement-decoupled reference, once initialized from an accepted
anchor, propagates without access to future measurements. The normal
prediction loss is therefore formulated directly on the free-rollout
trajectory:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal L_{\rm pred}
=\sum_s\sum_{\ell=1}^{\ell_{\rm tr}}\big(&
\lambda_z\norm{\bm z_{s+\ell}^{\rm enc}
-\bm z_{s+\ell\mid s}^{\rm roll}}_1\\
&+
\lambda_y\norm{\bm y_{s+\ell}
-\bm y_{s+\ell\mid s}^{\rm roll}}_1
\big).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:prediction_loss}
\end{equation}
The $\ell_1$ norm is chosen over the more common $\ell_2$ norm because it
produces a tighter elementwise propagation bound in Corollary~\ref{cor:bound}
and aligns with the $\ell_1$ pattern distances used later for
isolation. The two loss weights $\lambda_z$ and $\lambda_y$ control the
trade-off between latent-state accuracy and output reconstruction fidelity.

