decision module produces a score, a dynamic threshold, and either a unique
group or a compatible set.

\subsection{Measurement-Decoupled Reference and Unified Residual}
A single residual object serves training, short-horizon monitoring, and
long-reference monitoring, differing only in the choice of the rollout start
$s$. For any start index $s$, the rollout \eqref{eq:free_rollout} defines the
lifted residual
\begin{equation}
\bm e_{k\mid s}^z=\bm z_k^{\rm enc}-\bm z_{k\mid s}^{\rm roll},
\qquad k\geq s,
\label{eq:start_residual}
\end{equation}
and the instantaneous output-consistency residual is
\begin{equation}
\bm e_k^y=\bm y_k-
\mathcal D_\Theta(\bm z_k^{\rm enc},\bm u_k).
\label{eq:output_residual}
\end{equation}
The same lifted residual \eqref{eq:start_residual} appears in the training
loss \eqref{eq:prediction_loss}; online, only the start index changes. Setting
$s=k-\ell_{\rm sh}$ gives a short rolling residual over the most recent
$\ell_{\rm sh}$ samples; setting $s=s_k^{\mathfrak n}$, where
$s_k^{\mathfrak n}$ is the latest delay-confirmed normal anchor (the
confirmation procedure is defined below), gives the long-reference residual.
The long reference state and its age are defined as
\begin{equation}
\bm z_k^{\mathfrak n}=\bm z_{k\mid s_k^{\mathfrak n}}^{\rm roll},\qquad
\ell_k^{\mathfrak n}=k-s_k^{\mathfrak n}.
\label{eq:normal_reference}
\end{equation}
The joint residual stacks all three components:
\begin{equation}
\bm e_k=
\begin{bmatrix}
(\bm e_k^y)^\T&
(\bm e_{k\mid k-\ell_{\rm sh}}^z)^\T&
(\bm e_{k\mid s_k^{\mathfrak n}}^z)^\T
\end{bmatrix}^{\T}.
\label{eq:joint_residual}
\end{equation}

Anchor management follows a delay-confirmation discipline. An encoded state is
not immediately accepted as a normal anchor. It is first stored as a candidate
and committed only after $\ell_{\rm cf}$ consecutive warning-free samples
elapse. During warning and alarm intervals, the accepted anchor is frozen and
\eqref{eq:free_rollout} continues without measurement updates, preventing
abnormal measurements from contaminating the reference.

\begin{proposition}[Delayed anchor acceptance]
\label{prop:anchor}
Assume that every abnormal trajectory belonging to a specified detectable
class triggers a warning within at most $\ell_{\rm det}$ samples of its onset.
If $\ell_{\rm cf}\geq\ell_{\rm det}$, no candidate generated at or after that
onset is committed before the warning.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Commitment of a candidate requires $\ell_{\rm cf}$ consecutive warning-free
samples. Under the stated premise, a warning occurs no later than $\ell_{\rm
det}\leq\ell_{\rm cf}$ samples after onset, so any post-onset candidate fails
the acceptance condition.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:anchor} is therefore a conditional guarantee: faults
that produce no detectable change in the available input--output information
cannot be covered. The discipline is re-applied after every alarm: the method
waits at least $\ell_h$ samples so that the encoder history no longer spans
the flagged interval, and then repeats the delay-confirmation procedure.

To characterize how the start-indexed residual evolves, two intermediate
quantities that isolate distinct sources of model mismatch are introduced:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm\nu_k
&=\bm z_{k+1}^{\rm enc}
-\mathcal T_\Theta(\bm z_k^{\rm enc},\bm u_k;\bm\chi_k),\\
\bm\delta_{k\mid s}^{\chi}
&=\mathcal T_\Theta(\bm z_k^{\rm enc},\bm u_k;\bm\chi_k)
-\mathcal T_\Theta(\bm z_k^{\rm enc},\bm u_k;
\bm\chi_{k\mid s}^{\rm roll}).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:innovation_context}
\end{equation}
The innovation $\bm\nu_k$ captures the one-step inconsistency of the encoded
state with the transition model evaluated at the encoding context; the context
mismatch $\bm\delta_{k\mid s}^{\chi}$ measures the effect of replacing the
encoding context $\bm\chi_k$ (which benefits from the true measurement
history) with the rollout context $\bm\chi_{k\mid s}^{\rm roll}$ (which uses
only self-predicted outputs).

\begin{theorem}[Exact start-indexed residual propagation]
\label{thm:exact}
Assume $\mathcal T_\Theta$ is continuously differentiable in $\bm z$ on the
line segment joining $\bm z_{k\mid s}^{\rm roll}$ and $\bm z_k^{\rm enc}$.
Then the lifted residual obeys the exact one-step recursion
\begin{equation}
\bm e_{k+1\mid s}^z
=\overline{\bm\Gamma}_{k\mid s}\bm e_{k\mid s}^z
+\bm\nu_k+\bm\delta_{k\mid s}^{\chi},
\label{eq:exact_recursion}
\end{equation}
where the averaged Jacobian is
\begin{equation}
\overline{\bm\Gamma}_{k\mid s}=
\int_0^1
\bm\Gamma_{z,k}\!\left(
\bm z_{k\mid s}^{\rm roll}
+\tau\bm e_{k\mid s}^z\right)\,\mathrm d\tau.
\label{eq:averaged_jacobian}
\end{equation}
Consequently, the finite-horizon propagation admits the explicit expansion
\begin{equation}
\bm e_{k\mid s}^z=
\bm\Phi(k,s)\bm e_{s\mid s}^z+
\sum_{j=s}^{k-1}\bm\Phi(k,j+1)
(\bm\nu_j+\bm\delta_{j\mid s}^{\chi}),
\label{eq:finite_propagation}
\end{equation}
with the ordered product $\bm\Phi(k,j)=
\overline{\bm\Gamma}_{k-1\mid s}\cdots
\overline{\bm\Gamma}_{j\mid s}$ and the boundary condition
$\bm\Phi(j,j)=\bm I$.
\end{theorem}

The proof proceeds by adding and subtracting the transition evaluated at the
encoding context $\bm\chi_k$ and at the rollout context
$\bm\chi_{k\mid s}^{\rm roll}$, both at the encoded state $\bm z_k^{\rm enc}$,
followed by the integral mean-value identity; the complete derivation is given
in Appendix~A. The recursion consequently exposes the two sources of
finite-horizon forcing: the one-step normal-model inconsistency $\bm\nu_k$
inherent in the finite-history encoding, and the context mismatch
$\bm\delta_{k\mid s}^{\chi}$ that accumulates with the attention memory length.

\subsection{Fuzzy-Structure-Dependent Normal Propagation}
Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} reduces the residual evolution to the product of
averaged Jacobians and the accumulated forcing. Obtaining a computable bound
on the Jacobian requires analyzing how the complete T--S fuzzy structure---not
merely the weighted local matrices---responds to changes in the lifted state.

For compactness, define the local next-state value $\bm v_{i,k}=\bm
A_i\bm z_k+\bm B_i\bm u_k$ and its membership-weighted average $\overline{\bm
v}_k=\sum_i\omega_{i,k}\bm v_{i,k}$. From the membership definition
\eqref{eq:fuzzy_weights}, the gradient of each rule's metric energy with
respect to the scheduling vector is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm\gamma_{i,k}^{\rho}
&:=\nabla_{\bm\rho}\varphi_{i,k}
=-\bm\Pi_i(\bm\rho_k-\bm c_i),\\
\overline{\bm\gamma}_k^{\rho}
&:=\sum_j\omega_{j,k}\bm\gamma_{j,k}^{\rho},\\
\nabla_{\bm\rho}\omega_{i,k}
&=\omega_{i,k}
(\bm\gamma_{i,k}^{\rho}-\overline{\bm\gamma}_k^{\rho}).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:weight_gradient}
\end{equation}
The difference $\bm\gamma_{i,k}^{\rho}-\overline{\bm\gamma}_k^{\rho}$
quantifies how strongly rule $i$'s local metric gradient deviates from the
membership-weighted average. This term is small in regions where a single rule
dominates and becomes significant in transition zones where multiple rules
compete.

\begin{theorem}[Complete fuzzy lifted-state Jacobian]
\label{thm:jacobian}
For fixed $\bm u_k$ and $\bm\chi_k$, the Jacobian of the interpolated
transition \eqref{eq:fuzzy_transition} with respect to $\bm z$ decomposes as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm\Gamma_{z,k}
={}&\sum_{i=1}^{m_r}\omega_{i,k}\bm A_i\\
&+\sum_{i=1}^{m_r}\omega_{i,k}
(\bm v_{i,k}-\overline{\bm v}_k)
(\bm\gamma_{i,k}^{\rho}
-\overline{\bm\gamma}_k^{\rho})^\T\bm W_z .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:complete_jacobian}
\end{equation}
Moreover, its infinity norm satisfies the implementable bound
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\norm{\bm\Gamma_{z,k}}_\infty\leq\kappa_k:={}&
\sum_i\omega_{i,k}\norm{\bm A_i}_\infty\\
&+\norm{\bm W_z}_\infty
\sum_i\omega_{i,k}
\norm{\bm v_{i,k}-\overline{\bm v}_k}_\infty\\
&\hspace{14mm}\times
\norm{\bm\gamma_{i,k}^{\rho}
-\overline{\bm\gamma}_k^{\rho}}_1 .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:jacobian_bound}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

Equation~\eqref{eq:complete_jacobian} separates two contributions that arise
from distinct structural mechanisms of the fuzzy interpolation. The first sum
is the direct interpolation of the local dynamics matrices---the term that
would appear if membership weights were fixed. The second sum captures the
variation of the normalized firing strengths with respect to the lifted state.
This term becomes large only when three conditions coincide: multiple rules
have non-negligible weights (rule overlap), their local next-state
predictions differ substantially, and the scheduling vector lies in a
direction that redistributes weight among those rules. The proof of the
decomposition and the derivation of the induced-norm bound are given in
Appendix~B.

Equation~\eqref{eq:jacobian_bound} supplies the structural component of the
propagation bound, while the unresolved remainder---arising from finite-data
approximation, unmodeled disturbances, and encoder errors---is represented by
an empirically calibrated forcing term.

\begin{assumption}[Normal residual forcing]
\label{ass:normal}
On a prescribed normal operating domain, known nonnegative values
$\overline{\varepsilon}_k$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
\norm{\bm\nu_k+\bm\delta_{k\mid s}^{\chi}}_\infty
\leq\overline{\varepsilon}_k
\label{eq:forcing_bound}
\end{equation}
on the calibrated normal event.
\end{assumption}

The values in Assumption~\ref{ass:normal} are not claimed as deterministic
global bounds derived from finite data. The structural contribution comes from
\eqref{eq:hard_bounds}--\eqref{eq:jacobian_bound}; the unresolved normal
remainder is calibrated on held-out normal blocks in Section~IV.

\begin{corollary}[Finite-horizon normal residual bound]
\label{cor:bound}
If $\beta_{s\mid s}\geq\norm{\bm e_{s\mid s}^z}_\infty$ and the scalar
sequence satisfies the linear recursion
\begin{equation}
\beta_{k+1\mid s}=\kappa_k\beta_{k\mid s}
+\overline{\varepsilon}_k,
\label{eq:beta_recursion}
\end{equation}
then $\norm{\bm e_{k\mid s}^z}_\infty\leq\beta_{k\mid s}$ holds while the
trajectory remains in the prescribed normal domain. For a given reference
tolerance $\overline{\beta}$, the reliable horizon is
\begin{equation}
\ell_{\rm ref}(s)=
\max\left\{\ell:\beta_{s+j\mid s}\leq\overline{\beta},
\ 1\leq j\leq\ell\right\}.
\label{eq:reliable_horizon}
\end{equation}
\end{corollary}

All quantities in \eqref{eq:jacobian_bound} involve only matrix products,
elementwise absolute values, and row or column sums---operations that are
efficient and compatible with first-order automatic differentiation. The
empirical training counterpart of \eqref{eq:beta_recursion},
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\rm nr}=
\sum_s\sum_{\ell=1}^{\ell_{\rm tr}}
\beta_{s+\ell\mid s}^{\rm emp},
\label{eq:normal_propagation_loss}
\end{equation}
serves as a normal-reference propagation regularizer. By itself, it does not
constitute a claim of improved physical fault sensitivity; its role is to
control the normal side of residual separation.

\subsection{Structured Basis-Perturbation Residual Learning}
Reducing the normal propagation loss \eqref{eq:normal_propagation_loss}
controls only one side of the diagnostic problem. The regularizer
\eqref{eq:beta_recursion} penalizes large values of the propagated bound
$\beta_{k\mid s}$, but this bound enters the training objective
multiplicatively with the Jacobian norm $\kappa_k$ and additively with the
forcing bound $\overline{\varepsilon}_k$. Because the same matrices $\bm A_i$,
$\bm B_i$, and $\bm W_z$ that determine $\kappa_k$ also determine the
model's sensitivity to input and state perturbations, a parameter
configuration that yields a small $\beta_{k\mid s}$ may simultaneously
produce a small response to any perturbation---normal or abnormal. This is the
well-known tension between nominal robustness and fault sensitivity in
residual-based diagnosis: any mechanism that attenuates normal propagation
also risks attenuating the abnormal response unless a separate diagnostic
objective counterbalances it. Without physical fault data or a
known fault direction, a diagnostic training signal must therefore be
generated from structure that is explicitly available within the learned
model.

To this end, define the model-coordinate perturbation group set
\begin{equation}
\mathbb G_{\rm b}=\{\mathrm u,\mathrm y,\mathrm z\},
\label{eq:group_set}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm u$, $\mathrm y$, and $\mathrm z$ identify the input,
measurement, and lifted-coordinate branches, respectively. These labels
specify where a perturbation is inserted in the learned architecture; they are
not physical actuator, sensor, or process categories.

At a sampled start $s$, one standard basis vector and a signed amplitude
$\alpha$ are selected. The perturbation is injected into exactly one branch
while the rest of the model remains frozen:
\begin{itemize}
\item The input branch replaces $\bm u_s$ by $\bm u_s+\alpha\bm q_j^u$ in the
model rollout, simulating a perturbation applied to the recorded command.
\item The measurement branch replaces $\bm y_s$ by $\bm y_s+\alpha\bm q_j^y$
in the current output residual and in all subsequent causal histories that
include this sample.
\item The lifted branch adds the perturbation directly to the one-step
transition:
\begin{equation}
\bm z_{s+1}^{(\mathrm z,j,\alpha)}
=\mathcal T_\Theta(\bm z_s^{\rm enc},\bm u_s;\bm\chi_s)
+\alpha\bm q_j^z.
\label{eq:lifted_perturbation}
\end{equation}
\end{itemize}
After the injection, every branch follows the same frozen model and reference
logic as the unperturbed normal branch. Denote the unperturbed joint residual
at sample $t$ by $\bm e_t^{\mathfrak n}$.

For a response horizon $\ell_{\rm b}$, the perturbed and normal residual
stacks are differenced to isolate the perturbation response:
\begin{equation}
\Delta\bm e_{s,\ell_{\rm b}}^{g,j,\alpha}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
(\bm e_s^{g,j,\alpha}-\bm e_s^{\mathfrak n})^\T&
\cdots&
(\bm e_{s+\ell_{\rm b}-1}^{g,j,\alpha}
-\bm e_{s+\ell_{\rm b}-1}^{\mathfrak n})^\T
\end{bmatrix}^{\T}.
\label{eq:response_increment}
\end{equation}
The amplitude-normalized sensitivity and the unit-$\ell_1$ response pattern
are defined as
\begin{equation}
\eta_{g,j,\alpha}^{\rm rsp}=
\frac{\norm{\Delta\bm e_{s,\ell_{\rm b}}^{g,j,\alpha}}_1}
{\abs{\alpha}+\epsilon_{\rm reg}},
\qquad
\bm p_{g,j,\alpha}=
\frac{\abs{\Delta\bm e_{s,\ell_{\rm b}}^{g,j,\alpha}}}
{\norm{\Delta\bm e_{s,\ell_{\rm b}}^{g,j,\alpha}}_1+\epsilon_{\rm reg}},
\label{eq:response_pattern}
\end{equation}
where the absolute value in the numerator of the pattern is elementwise and
$\epsilon_{\rm reg}>0$ provides numerical stabilization.

With these quantities, the diagnostic side of training pursues two objectives
that operate jointly with the normal-propagation regularizer: the sensitivity
loss, which encourages each group's perturbation to produce a response of at
least a target magnitude, and the separation loss, which enforces that
response patterns from different groups remain distinguishable in $\ell_1$
distance by at least a margin $\eta_{\rm iso}$:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal L_{\rm sen}
&=\sum_{g,j,\alpha}
\left[\underline{\eta}_g-\eta_{g,j,\alpha}^{\rm rsp}\right]_+^2,\\
\mathcal L_{\rm iso}
&=\sum_{g\ne\bar g}
\left[
\eta_{\rm iso}
-\norm{\bm p_{g,j,\alpha}
-\bm p_{\bar g,\bar j,\bar\alpha}}_1
\right]_+^2.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:response_losses}
\end{equation}
The complete training objective is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L=
\mathcal L_{\rm pred}
+\lambda_{\rm nr}\mathcal L_{\rm nr}
+\lambda_{\rm sen}\mathcal L_{\rm sen}
+\lambda_{\rm iso}\mathcal L_{\rm iso}
+\lambda_{\rm bal}\mathcal L_{\rm bal},
\label{eq:complete_loss}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal L_{\rm bal}$ penalizes only rule occupancies that fall below a
prescribed minimum; it does not enforce uniform rule usage. Each mini-batch
samples one coordinate from each group, so the additional computation consists
of three ordinary forward rollouts and first-order backpropagation. No
Hessian, eigendecomposition, singular-value decomposition, matrix inverse, or
online optimization is introduced by this construction.

The additive form of \eqref{eq:complete_loss} mirrors the modular design of
the method: each loss targets one requirement of the monitoring objective, and
the weights $\lambda_{\rm nr}$, $\lambda_{\rm sen}$, $\lambda_{\rm iso}$, and
$\lambda_{\rm bal}$ govern the relative emphasis among the prediction,
propagation, and diagnostic objectives, with larger values placing stronger
weight on the corresponding property.

Having constructed the measurement-decoupled reference, the normal propagation
bound, and the structured perturbation responses, we now formulate the online
procedure that consumes these frozen objects to produce detection alarms and
isolation decisions.

\section{Input-Conditioned Detection and Set-Valued Isolation}
\subsection{Input-Conditioned Residual Standardization}
The raw joint residual \eqref{eq:joint_residual} follows a distribution that
shifts with the realized command, the command variation, the active fuzzy
operating region, and the age of the measurement-decoupled reference. Applying
a fixed threshold across these changing conditions would confound normal
operating-point variation with abnormality. The following standardization
absorbs these dependencies into a condition-dependent scale before a scalar
score is formed.

The condition descriptor collects the quantities that govern the residual
distribution:
\begin{equation}
\bm\upsilon_k=\bm u_k-\bm u_{k-1},\qquad
\bm\zeta_k=
\begin{bmatrix}
(\bm\omega_k^{\mathfrak n})^\T&
\bm u_k^\T&
\bm\upsilon_k^\T&
\ell_k^{\mathfrak n}
\end{bmatrix}^{\T},
\label{eq:descriptor}
\end{equation}
where $\bm\omega_k^{\mathfrak n}$ contains the firing strengths evaluated
along the normal reference $\bm z_k^{\mathfrak n}$ and its predicted context.
For each fuzzy rule, normal training blocks supply a local conditional mean
$\bm\mu_i(\bm\zeta_k)$ and a positive elementwise scale
$\bm\sigma_i(\bm\zeta_k)$. The mixture moments incorporate both the
within-rule uncertainty and the between-rule dispersion:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm\mu_k&=\sum_i\omega_{i,k}^{\mathfrak n}\bm\mu_i,\\
\bm\sigma_k^{\odot2}
&=\sum_i\omega_{i,k}^{\mathfrak n}
\left[
\bm\sigma_i^{\odot2}
+(\bm\mu_i-\bm\mu_k)^{\odot2}
\right]+\epsilon_\sigma\1 .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:mixture_moments}
\end{equation}
The between-rule term $(\bm\mu_i-\bm\mu_k)^{\odot2}$ prevents the conditional
variance from being underestimated at rule boundaries, where the residual mean
can shift appreciably between adjacent operating regimes, and the additive
floor $\epsilon_\sigma\1$ keeps every element of $\bm\sigma_k^{\odot2}$
positive. Let $\bm\sigma_k$ denote the elementwise square root of
$\bm\sigma_k^{\odot2}$ in \eqref{eq:mixture_moments}; elementwise
standardization yields
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\bm e}_k=
(\bm e_k-\bm\mu_k)\oslash\bm\sigma_k.
\label{eq:standardized_residual}
\end{equation}

For each window length $\ell$ drawn from a predeclared finite list
$\{\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_{\max}\}$, the accumulated $\ell_1$ score is
\begin{equation}
\varsigma_{k,\ell}=
\sum_{j=k-\ell+1}^{k}\norm{\widetilde{\bm e}_j}_1.
\label{eq:window_score}
\end{equation}
A held-out normal calibration split is partitioned by predeclared fuzzy
operating cells and reference-age intervals. Within each cell, nonoverlapping
blocks calibrate the upper quantile $\tau_{\rm cal}(\bm\zeta_k,\ell)$. The
propagated deterministic component from \eqref{eq:beta_recursion}, after the
same elementwise scaling, contributes $\tau_{\rm
prop}(\beta_{k\mid s_k^{\mathfrak n}},\ell)$. The online threshold combines
both sources:
\begin{equation}
\tau_{k,\ell}=
\tau_{\rm cal}(\bm\zeta_k,\ell)
+\tau_{\rm prop}(\beta_{k\mid s_k^{\mathfrak n}},\ell).
\label{eq:dynamic_threshold}
\end{equation}
The calibration term absorbs the unresolved random remainder; the propagation
term tracks the accumulating reference drift. Together they produce a
threshold that adapts to the input, input variation, fuzzy region, and
reference age without online re-estimation. In practice, the two components
play complementary roles: $\tau_{\rm cal}$ dominates shortly after anchor
acceptance, when the reference drift is small and the residual distribution is
determined primarily by normal modeling error; $\tau_{\rm prop}$ grows with
reference age $\ell_k^{\mathfrak n}$ and eventually overtakes the calibration
term, signaling that the reference has accumulated enough drift to warrant
reconfirmation.

The multiscale statistic takes the maximum normalized score over the declared
window family:
\begin{equation}
\varsigma_k^{\max}=
\max_{\ell\in\{\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_{\max}\}}
\frac{\varsigma_{k,\ell}}{\tau_{k,\ell}}.
\label{eq:multiscale}
\end{equation}
Its final decision level is calibrated directly on the maximum score, avoiding
the multiplicity correction that separate per-window tests would require.
Under serial dependence, the finite-sample coverage interpretation is
blockwise rather than samplewise, conforming to the exchangeable-block
calibration framework \cite{vovk2005,xu2021}.

\subsection{Sufficient Detection Condition}
The normal-propagation regularizer \eqref{eq:normal_propagation_loss} and the
structured response losses \eqref{eq:response_losses} act on opposite sides of
the same residual. The following proposition makes explicit why both are
necessary and how they combine to produce a detectable margin.

\begin{proposition}[Residual-space detection]
\label{prop:detection}
For a selected window, suppose a normal standardized residual block satisfies
$\norm{\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,\mathfrak n}}_1\leq\tau_{k,\ell}$ on the
calibrated normal event. Let the corresponding faulty residual block be
$\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,\mathfrak f}
=\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,\mathfrak n}
+\Delta\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,\mathfrak f}$, and suppose a structured
perturbation produces the learned response
$\Delta\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,g,j,\alpha}$ with
$\norm{\Delta\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,\mathfrak f}
-\Delta\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,g,j,\alpha}}_1\leq\epsilon_g$. If
\begin{equation}
\norm{\Delta\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,g,j,\alpha}}_1
>2\tau_{k,\ell}+\epsilon_g,
\label{eq:detection_condition}
\end{equation}
then the corresponding residual block exceeds $\tau_{k,\ell}$ on that event.
\end{proposition}

The proof follows from the reverse triangle inequality:
$\norm{\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,\mathfrak f}}_1
\geq\norm{\Delta\widetilde{\bm e}^{\,g,j,\alpha}}_1
-\epsilon_g-\tau_{k,\ell}
>\tau_{k,\ell}$. Assuming the trained responses satisfy $\norm{\Delta\widetilde{\bm
e}^{\,g,j,\alpha}}_1 \geq\underline{\eta}_g\abs{\alpha}$ after the
standardization of \eqref{eq:standardized_residual}, the condition translates
into a sufficient amplitude requirement:
\begin{equation}
\abs{\alpha}>
\frac{2\tau_{k,\ell}+\epsilon_g}{\underline{\eta}_g}.
\label{eq:amplitude_condition}
\end{equation}
This is a residual-model conclusion, not a physical minimum-fault claim. It
exposes the two factors that the training objective can control: reducing
normal propagation lowers the threshold $\tau_{k,\ell}$, and increasing the
structured perturbation response raises the sensitivity $\underline{\eta}_g$.

\subsection{Unique-or-Set-Valued Isolation}
After a detection is declared, the observed residual increment is normalized
to a unit-$\ell_1$ pattern by the same procedure as
\eqref{eq:response_pattern}. For each group $g$, let $\delta_g(k)$ be the
smallest $\ell_1$ distance from the observed pattern to the patterns stored
during training rollouts for that group. The compatible set collects every
group whose distance falls within a calibrated tolerance:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb G_k^{\rm cmp}=
\left\{g\in\mathbb G_{\rm b}:
\delta_g(k)\leq\tau_{\rm iso}\right\}.
\label{eq:candidate_set}
\end{equation}
A unique group is returned only when $\mathbb G_k^{\rm cmp}$ contains exactly
one element and the distance to the second-closest group exceeds a calibrated
ambiguity margin. When neither condition holds, the observed residual pattern
does not uniquely implicate one insertion site, and the method reports the
full compatible set.

\begin{proposition}[Sufficient group separation]
\label{prop:isolation}
Suppose the observed normalized response lies within $\delta_{\rm obs}$ of a
stored pattern generated from the true insertion group. If every stored
pattern belonging to a different group is at an $\ell_1$ distance greater than
$2\delta_{\rm obs}$ from that stored pattern, nearest-pattern classification
recovers the generating group.
\end{proposition}

The result follows from the triangle inequality. When its premise is
unsatisfied---either because the observed pattern falls near a group boundary or
because the perturbation responses of two groups overlap in the residual
space---the compatible set \eqref{eq:candidate_set} is returned. The
labels $\mathrm u$, $\mathrm y$, and $\mathrm z$ indicate compatibility with a
perturbation insertion site in the learned architecture. Without an
independently verified mapping from model coordinates to physical components,
they must not be reinterpreted as unique actuator, sensor, or process fault
identifications.

\subsection{Offline and Online Algorithms}
The offline procedure, summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:offline}, consumes
disjoint normal training and calibration blocks. All learned parameters,
statistics, thresholds, and response patterns are frozen before online
deployment.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Normal-Data Offline Design}
\label{alg:offline}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Disjoint normal training and calibration blocks
\State Train $\mathcal H_\Theta$, $\mathcal T_\Theta$, and
$\mathcal D_\Theta$ by free rollouts using \eqref{eq:prediction_loss}.
\State Apply \eqref{eq:metric_parameterization} and \eqref{eq:hard_bounds};
compute \eqref{eq:jacobian_bound} and \eqref{eq:normal_propagation_loss}.
\State Sample one coordinate from each group in \eqref{eq:group_set};
optimize \eqref{eq:complete_loss} with ordinary forward branches.
\State Freeze all model and fuzzy-rule parameters.
\State Estimate \eqref{eq:mixture_moments} and calibrate
\eqref{eq:dynamic_threshold} on held-out nonoverlapping normal blocks.
\State Store response patterns and calibrate the ambiguity margin for
\eqref{eq:candidate_set}.
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Online, the encoder continuously reads the strictly past history while the
measurement-decoupled reference either advances from its accepted anchor or
awaits delayed reconfirmation. Algorithm~\ref{alg:online} summarizes the
per-sample decision loop.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Online Monitoring}
\label{alg:online}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Frozen model, normal statistics, thresholds, and response patterns
\For{each sample $k$}
\State Compute $\bm z_k^{\rm enc}$ and $\bm e_k^y$ from the data branch.
\State Propagate the short rollout and the measurement-decoupled reference.
\State Form $\bm e_k$ by \eqref{eq:joint_residual} and
$\bm\zeta_k$ by \eqref{eq:descriptor}.
\State Compute \eqref{eq:standardized_residual}--\eqref{eq:multiscale}.
\If{$\varsigma_k^{\max}$ exceeds its calibrated decision level}
\State Freeze anchor acceptance and report an alarm.
\State Return $\mathbb G_k^{\rm cmp}$ by \eqref{eq:candidate_set}.
\Else
\State Continue delay-confirmed anchor management.
\EndIf
\State After an alarm, wait $\ell_h$ samples and repeat delay confirmation
before accepting a new anchor.
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The online stage performs only encoder and model forward passes, elementwise
standardization, vector norms, and the lookup-table threshold and score
evaluations of \eqref{eq:dynamic_threshold} and \eqref{eq:multiscale}. Fuzzy
centers, metrics, network weights, and thresholds remain frozen; no online
update, reclustering, matrix decomposition, or optimization is required.

\section{Conclusion}
This paper formulated normal-data-only nonlinear monitoring as three aligned
problems whose solutions are structurally interdependent. Preventing new
measurements from updating the normal reference, controlling the free-rollout
drift that this decoupling exposes, and making residual-space decisions without
physical fault priors cannot be addressed in isolation---each solution constrains
and enables the next.

A measurement-decoupled attention--Koopman--T--S reference and a start-indexed
residual were constructed so that one and the same residual object serves
free-rollout training, short-horizon monitoring, and long-reference
evaluation, avoiding the discrepancy that would arise if training and online
operation used different residual definitions. The complete fuzzy Jacobian
decomposition then linked normal propagation to four structurally distinct
factors---local dynamics, rule overlap, local-model disparity, and scheduling
sensitivity---and supplied an implementable infinity-norm bound that requires
only elementwise operations and row or column sums. Structured
model-coordinate perturbations, injected separately into the input,
measurement, and lifted branches, complemented the normal-propagation objective
by providing a diagnostic training signal that increases residual sensitivity
and intergroup separation without physical fault data.

On the decision side, input-, fuzzy-region-, and reference-age-conditioned
standardization produced a dynamic detection rule, with sufficient
residual-space conditions that expose the complementary roles of propagation
reduction and response amplification. The isolation output is deliberately
conservative: when the observed residual pattern does not uniquely implicate
one insertion location, the method returns a compatible set rather than an
unsupported singleton.

The method does not claim unique physical fault identification: such a claim
requires additional actuation measurements, fault-channel knowledge, or a
verified mapping from lifted coordinates to physical components.

